{
  "dom_id": "step-q51328",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "The altitude $h(t)$ of a falling object object, measured in meters, is recorded every second for an interval of six seconds. The values of $h(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $h(t)$ | $200$ | $195$ | $181$ | $159$ | $128$ | $91$ | $51$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of the altitude when $t=3$ using a backward difference approximation.",
  "worked_solution": "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(t)\u2248\\frac{h(t)-h(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=3.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{\u2032}(3) & \u2248\\frac{h(3)-h(3-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{h(3)-h(2)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{159-181}{1} \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the rate of change of the altitude is $-22\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$",
